WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.075 --> 00:00:01.400
(chill music)

00:00:01.400 --> 00:00:02.680
- [Narrator] You can
tell a lot about a person

00:00:02.680 --> 00:00:03.990
based on their shoes.

00:00:03.990 --> 00:00:06.223
And today, there's a ton of options.

00:00:07.200 --> 00:00:11.070
In 2018, footwear was a
$250 billion industry.

00:00:11.070 --> 00:00:14.070
With over 24 million
shoes produced globally.

00:00:14.070 --> 00:00:15.560
Just look at Kanye.

00:00:15.560 --> 00:00:18.480
His shoe and apparel line
is valued at $1 billion.

00:00:20.220 --> 00:00:21.610
The problem is, lots of shoes,

00:00:21.610 --> 00:00:23.960
especially sneakers, aren't made to last.

00:00:23.960 --> 00:00:26.560
They're made of plastic
and we can't recycle them.

00:00:26.560 --> 00:00:28.383
So a lot of 'em end up as trash.

00:00:29.960 --> 00:00:34.073
So the question, can
sneakers become sustainable?

00:00:35.277 --> 00:00:37.944
(birds calling)

00:00:42.150 --> 00:00:45.730
The average American, in 2018,
bought seven pairs of shoes.

00:00:45.730 --> 00:00:47.140
But let's focus on the sneaker,

00:00:47.140 --> 00:00:48.780
which wasn't always so popular.

00:00:48.780 --> 00:00:49.980
Here's where it started.

00:00:51.733 --> 00:00:54.400
It's the late 1870s.
(peppy music)

00:00:54.400 --> 00:00:56.480
Lawn tennis becomes popular,

00:00:56.480 --> 00:00:59.130
which allows men and women to
compete against each other.

00:00:59.130 --> 00:01:01.163
Or tennis and chill.

00:01:02.010 --> 00:01:05.493
That game also created a
new must-have item, these.

00:01:09.948 --> 00:01:11.310
(upbeat music)

00:01:11.310 --> 00:01:12.930
Sports became really popular.

00:01:12.930 --> 00:01:16.240
Basketball, Babe Ruth, Jack Dempsey, golf.

00:01:16.240 --> 00:01:19.310
And by 1919, almost 20
million pairs of tennis shoes

00:01:19.310 --> 00:01:21.240
were being produced in the US.

00:01:21.240 --> 00:01:24.920
Brands like Keds, Converse, and PF Flyers

00:01:24.920 --> 00:01:27.420
launched the very beginning
of the sneaker market.

00:01:29.473 --> 00:01:32.306
(engine rumbling)

00:01:36.145 --> 00:01:38.310
This is Pensole Footwear Design Academy.

00:01:38.310 --> 00:01:40.230
And this is D'Wayne Edwards, its founder.

00:01:40.230 --> 00:01:42.520
- Shoes are very complicated.

00:01:42.520 --> 00:01:44.830
- [Narrator] Also one of the
first black footwear designers.

00:01:44.830 --> 00:01:48.563
His resume includes LA Gear,
Sketchers, Nike, and Jordan.

00:01:48.563 --> 00:01:51.040
(choir singing)

00:01:51.040 --> 00:01:52.520
- So plastics made their way

00:01:52.520 --> 00:01:55.910
onto sneakers in a few key areas.

00:01:55.910 --> 00:01:58.180
- [Narrator] First, in
the outsole for support.

00:01:58.180 --> 00:02:00.720
And then in the heel
counter for structure.

00:02:00.720 --> 00:02:03.840
In the 1970s jogging
becomes super popular.

00:02:03.840 --> 00:02:07.020
Companies introduce polyurethane
foam into the midsole.

00:02:07.020 --> 00:02:08.650
Which makes jogging more...

00:02:08.650 --> 00:02:10.180
- Uh, comfortable.

00:02:10.180 --> 00:02:11.230
- [Narrator] But they don't stop there.

00:02:11.230 --> 00:02:12.830
They start to focus on the athletes.

00:02:12.830 --> 00:02:15.870
- The goal was, if you can
make their footwear lighter,

00:02:15.870 --> 00:02:18.220
then you can make the athlete faster.

00:02:18.220 --> 00:02:19.440
- [Narrator] Molded EVA replaces

00:02:19.440 --> 00:02:21.570
polyurethane in the midsole, which...

00:02:21.570 --> 00:02:24.623
- Immediately cut the
weight down in half almost.

00:02:25.730 --> 00:02:28.060
(peppy music)

00:02:28.060 --> 00:02:30.570
- [Narrator] And almost
simultaneously synthetic leather

00:02:30.570 --> 00:02:32.730
is introduced into the
upper, which impacts

00:02:32.730 --> 00:02:36.257
the fit, weight, and maybe
more importantly, the design.

00:02:40.268 --> 00:02:42.851
(upbeat music)

00:02:47.490 --> 00:02:48.830
- My name is Nicoline van Enter.

00:02:48.830 --> 00:02:52.530
I am the founder and creative
director of the Footwearists.

00:02:52.530 --> 00:02:54.370
- [Narrator] Nicoline
is a footwear forecaster

00:02:54.370 --> 00:02:55.640
and shoe designer.

00:02:55.640 --> 00:02:58.670
Her job is to see trends
before they even happen.

00:02:58.670 --> 00:03:01.390
- Everybody could imagine
classic sneakers, for instance,

00:03:01.390 --> 00:03:04.940
that you collect from the
late 80s or early 90s,

00:03:04.940 --> 00:03:08.543
often now when you open the box
the sole just crumbles away.

00:03:08.543 --> 00:03:09.630
- [Man] Hey what's up everyone?

00:03:09.630 --> 00:03:10.850
Check it out.

00:03:10.850 --> 00:03:12.320
Midsole.

00:03:12.320 --> 00:03:16.130
You can see is just flaking off there.

00:03:16.130 --> 00:03:18.020
- That's essentially
what happens to plastics.

00:03:18.020 --> 00:03:19.630
And that's also why it's difficult to

00:03:19.630 --> 00:03:24.580
have a plastic shoe, recycle
it into another plastic shoe.

00:03:24.580 --> 00:03:26.470
- [Narrator] So right
now shoes are essentially

00:03:26.470 --> 00:03:28.680
a hodgepodge of materials,
which means when you

00:03:28.680 --> 00:03:31.220
wanna recycle an old shoe your options are

00:03:31.220 --> 00:03:34.470
donating it, grinding
it, or throwing it away.

00:03:34.470 --> 00:03:36.570
And that's a pretty short lifecycle.

00:03:36.570 --> 00:03:39.483
But, the future, it's
actually really exciting.

00:03:42.670 --> 00:03:44.340
As consumers, there are more

00:03:44.340 --> 00:03:47.100
sustainable options than ever before.

00:03:47.100 --> 00:03:50.890
The World Footwear 2030 report
predicts that sustainability

00:03:50.890 --> 00:03:53.420
will drive innovation in
the footwear industry.

00:03:53.420 --> 00:03:55.110
And it's already happening.

00:03:55.110 --> 00:03:57.010
Big brands are experimenting
with things like

00:03:57.010 --> 00:03:59.460
biofabrication, like using mushrooms

00:03:59.460 --> 00:04:01.210
to grow the materials for their shoes.

00:04:01.210 --> 00:04:04.210
And 3D printing, which
significantly reduces waste

00:04:04.210 --> 00:04:05.990
during the manufacturing process.

00:04:05.990 --> 00:04:08.680
One example of this is the
Adidas Futurecraft.Loop.

00:04:08.680 --> 00:04:09.680
Here's how it works.

00:04:09.680 --> 00:04:11.870
- If you have a shoe of only one material

00:04:11.870 --> 00:04:15.110
you can grind that up,
take it back to pellets,

00:04:15.110 --> 00:04:17.590
melt that again, and turn it back into

00:04:17.590 --> 00:04:20.140
the same TPU that the shoe was made of.

00:04:20.140 --> 00:04:21.610
- [Narrator] But
companies still don't know

00:04:21.610 --> 00:04:24.230
how many times that
process can be repeated.

00:04:24.230 --> 00:04:26.930
Another consideration,
can a sustainable shoe

00:04:26.930 --> 00:04:29.350
still appeal to sneaker culture?

00:04:29.350 --> 00:04:31.960
- Sustainability, right now,

00:04:31.960 --> 00:04:33.720
does not have a design language.

00:04:33.720 --> 00:04:35.937
You can hold up a sustainable material

00:04:35.937 --> 00:04:39.430
and a non-sustainable material
in the form of a synthetic,

00:04:39.430 --> 00:04:43.490
a textile, a leather, a plastic, a foam,

00:04:43.490 --> 00:04:45.770
and not be able to tell a difference.

00:04:45.770 --> 00:04:47.200
That's a problem.

00:04:47.200 --> 00:04:51.290
If you want consumers to
truly embrace sustainability

00:04:51.290 --> 00:04:54.350
you have to win the aesthetic game.

00:04:54.350 --> 00:04:57.570
And the aesthetic game is
allowing sustainability

00:04:57.570 --> 00:05:00.153
to have its own natural aesthetic.

00:05:01.190 --> 00:05:02.610
- [Narrator] So back to the question,

00:05:02.610 --> 00:05:05.270
can sneakers become sustainable?

00:05:05.270 --> 00:05:08.040
It's going to come down
to how much companies

00:05:08.040 --> 00:05:11.220
are willing to invest,
what consumers want.

00:05:11.220 --> 00:05:13.340
And if technology can drive the change

00:05:13.340 --> 00:05:15.000
that will give us a material that's,

00:05:15.000 --> 00:05:17.594
well, better than plastic.

00:05:17.594 --> 00:05:20.094
(chill music)

